# Web Audio API Metronome Scheduling Algorithm

## Core Concept
The metronome uses a collaborative approach combining JavaScript timers with the Web Audio API's hardware clock. As the article explains, "a setTimeout timer that fires once every so often, and sets up Web Audio scheduling in the future for individual notes."

## Key Timing Values
- **setTimeout interval**: 25ms (how often the scheduler runs)
- **scheduleAheadTime**: 100ms (how far ahead notes are scheduled)
- **Overlap strategy**: Each scheduler call schedules events for the next 100ms, providing resilience against main-thread delays

## Scheduler Function
The core logic checks whether notes need scheduling:

```
while (nextNoteTime < audioContext.currentTime + scheduleAheadTime) {
  scheduleNote(current16thNote, nextNoteTime);
  nextNote();
}
```

This loop compares the next note's time against "the current audio hardware time" plus the lookahead buffer, scheduling any notes that fall within that window.

## nextNote() Function
This advances to the next sixteenth note:

```
var secondsPerBeat = 60.0 / tempo;
nextNoteTime += 0.25 * secondsPerBeat;
current16thNote++;
if (current16thNote == 16) {
  current16thNote = 0;
}
```

Crucially, this recalculates based on the current tempo, enabling real-time tempo changes.

## scheduleNote() Function
Uses Web Audio start/stop methods with precise timing:

```
var osc = audioContext.createOscillator();
osc.connect(audioContext.destination);
osc.frequency.value = [frequency based on beat];
osc.start(time);
osc.stop(time + noteLength);
```

## Why This Works
The algorithm avoids setTimeout's unreliability by using it only for scheduling decisions, not actual audio playback. The "large lookahead latency kept the timing going with no problem" even when setTimeout itself was delayed 50ms, because notes were pre-scheduled on the audio thread.

